<tool id="fileidentification" name="File Format Identification" version="@TOOL_VERSION@+galaxy@VERSION_SUFFIX@" profile="24.1">
    <description>Check multimedia files if they are corrupt or duplicated</description>
    <macros><import>macros.xml</import></macros>
    <expand macro="requirements"/>
    <!-- detect_errors="exit_code", not "aggressive": the tool exits 0 on data problems (corruption
         is a finding, not a failure), and a clean report legitimately contains the string "[ERROR]",
         which "aggressive" would misread as a tool failure. -->
    <command detect_errors="exit_code"><![CDATA[
unzip -q '$input' -d input_dir &&
/app/.venv/bin/python /app/identify.py ./input_dir --inspect > results.txt &&
cp input_dir/__fileidentification/*_report.json report.json
    ]]></command>
    <inputs>
        <param type="data" name="input" format="zip" label="ZIP containing the files to be analysed"/>
    </inputs>
    <outputs>
        <data name="report" format="json" from_work_dir="report.json"
              label="${tool.name} on ${on_string}: report (JSON)"/>
        <data name="out" format="txt" from_work_dir="results.txt"
              label="${tool.name} on ${on_string}: summary"/>
    </outputs>
    <tests>
        <test expect_num_outputs="2">
            <param name="input" value="test-data.zip"/>
            <output name="report" ftype="json">
                <assert_contents>
                    <!-- every format in the fixture is identified; PUIDs are pinned by pygfried in uv.lock -->
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/17"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/199"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/569"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/585"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/645"/>
                    <!-- the extension-less AVI is identified by content, not by name -->
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/5"/>
                    <has_text text="test-data/nested folder/testavi"/>
                    <!-- the corrupt mp4 is reported, at error level, by ffmpeg -->
                    <has_text text="test-data/corrupt.mp4"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="level" text="error"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="name" text="ffmpeg"/>
                    <!-- inspect mode is read-only: nothing is quarantined -->
                    <has_text text="&quot;removed&quot;: true" negate="true"/>
                    <!-- both md5-identical pairs are reported as duplicates -->
                    <has_text text="6c0cf64b97d107bdfae8d54ea4cd3375"/>
                    <has_text text="82b415572a47acd77156a1f69e61c75e"/>
                    <!-- the slim image is complete: no tool-missing failures on the office/pdf fixtures -->
                    <has_text text="No such file or directory" negate="true"/>
                </assert_contents>
            </output>
            <output name="out" ftype="txt">
                <!-- the summary's exact line-wrapping depends on terminal width, which is not fixed
                     in this environment, so assert shape (line count, key text), never exact bytes -->
                <assert_contents>
                    <has_text text="Duplicates"/>
                    <has_text text="Format Name"/>
                    <has_text text="test-data/corrupt.mp4"/>
                    <has_n_lines min="20" max="80"/>
                </assert_contents>
            </output>
        </test>
        <!-- second path: a clean input must not report errors or duplicates -->
        <test expect_num_outputs="2">
            <param name="input" value="test-data-clean.zip"/>
            <output name="report" ftype="json">
                <assert_contents>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/44"/>
                    <has_json_property_with_text property="processed_as" text="fmt/18"/>
                    <has_text text="&quot;level&quot;: &quot;error&quot;" negate="true"/>
                    <has_text text="&quot;duplicates&quot;: {}"/>
                </assert_contents>
            </output>
        </test>
    </tests>
    <help><![CDATA[
Do you have a huge number of multimedia files and you don't know if they are corrupt,
or if they have the correct extension?
This tool:

- gives you an overview of what file types there are
- checks if the extension of the files match their content
- checks if there are any duplicates
- checks if the content of the files is intact

Input: A ZIP containing the files to be analysed (may be a nested folder structure)

Output: A machine-readable JSON report, and a plain-text summary

A possible use case are digital preservation workflows,
where you want to make sure that you only preserve high-quality files for the future.

Supported file types: A wide range of image formats (pixel and vector), videos, audios, pdf, MS Office.

Note: The original fileidentification tool is more feature-rich.
In particular, it offers bulk conversion of files, which is currently not supported on Galaxy.

Find more information in `the GitHub repo <https://github.com/dasch-swiss/fileidentification>`_.
    ]]></help>
    <citations>
        <citation type="bibtex">
@misc{githubfileidentification,
  author = {{DaSCH, Swiss National Data and Service Center for the Humanities}},
  year = {2025},
  title = {Fileidentification - A CLI to Identify Multimedia File Formats and Bulk Convert Files},
  journal = {GitHub repository},
  url = {https://github.com/dasch-swiss/fileidentification},
}</citation>
    </citations>
</tool>
